---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'The problem' },
  { id: 'reality', label: 'What was in the way' },
  { id: 'built', label: 'What I built' },
  { id: 'rights', label: 'Decision rights' },
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why it matters' },
  { id: 'learned', label: 'What I learned' },
];
---

<CaseLayout slug="tamaloco" sections={sections}>
  <section id="problem">
    <h2><span class="n">01 / The problem</span>The recipe was never the missing piece.</h2>
    <p>
      Nadia can already make the food. What a business needed from her was everything around it: a
      name, a look, a story, prices, a public face, a position on how big this should get. All of it
      in a second language, on top of a life that was already full.
    </p>
    <p>
      The usual failure here is well meant. Somebody helpful arrives, builds a brand, hands it over,
      and it turns out to be their taste with her food attached. She doesn&rsquo;t recognise it, so
      she doesn&rsquo;t defend it, and the whole thing quietly stops.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reality">
    <h2><span class="n">02 / What was in the way</span>Three things, none of them the tamales.</h2>
    <ul class="rows">
      <li>
        <b>Language</b>
        Every brand worksheet I could find was in English. Asking somebody to describe how they want
        their food to feel, in their second language, gets you a shorter and flatter answer than the
        one they actually have.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>The blank page</b>
        &ldquo;What&rsquo;s your brand?&rdquo; is an unanswerable question. It asks for a finished
        opinion before anyone has had a chance to form one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Nobody had said who decides</b>
        Without that written down, one of two things happens: I stall on every small choice, or I
        start making them and she stops feeling like it&rsquo;s hers.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="built">
    <h2><span class="n">03 / What I built</span>A founder-vision kit, in her language.</h2>
    <p>
      A bilingual interview kit she can answer at her own pace. Spanish sets every line; English sits
      underneath it, smaller, for anyone else who has to read it later.
    </p>
    <p>It opens with three rules, and they are the whole design:</p>
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li lang="es"><strong>Nada se decide sin ti.</strong> Nothing gets locked without you.</li>
      <li lang="es">
        <strong>No tienes que ser la cara pública si no quieres.</strong> You do not have to be the
        public face unless you want to.
      </li>
      <li lang="es">
        <strong>El negocio debe caber en tu vida, no comerse tu vida.</strong> The business must fit
        your life, not swallow your life.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Then sections of real questions &mdash; heart and purpose, what someone should feel eating the
      food, how big this should ever get, how she feels about a loan. And a standing instruction
      that she can answer any way she wants: write a little, send an audio message, show a photo,
      pick from options, or just say <em>eso no me gusta</em> &mdash; I don&rsquo;t like that.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rights">
    <h2><span class="n">04 / Decision rights</span>Question 10 is the one that matters.</h2>
    <p>
      <strong lang="es">Cam puede decidir estas cosas sin preguntarme cada vez&hellip;</strong>
      &mdash; <em>Cam may decide these things without asking every time.</em> An open line she fills
      in herself.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is the most boring-looking question in the kit and the one that makes the rest of it work.
      Every implementation I have ever seen go wrong went wrong in the gap between what the person
      thought they had approved and what got built. Writing the boundary down, in her words, in her
      language, before anything is made, costs one line and removes that entire category of argument.
    </p>
    <p class="pull">
      Nadia decides the heart. I support the build. That sentence is printed on the bottom of every
      page of the kit, because it is the deal.
    </p>
    <p>
      The kit also carries a rule for me:
      <em lang="es">Cam puede traer ideas, ejemplos y opciones, pero primero debe escuchar.</em> I can
      bring ideas, examples and options &mdash; but I listen first, and if it doesn&rsquo;t feel like
      her, it changes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <h2><span class="n">05 / Why it matters</span>This is the job, with the software removed.</h2>
    <p>
      Strip out the code and an implementation is this: understand what the customer actually wants,
      in their language, at a pace they can manage; agree what you are allowed to decide; then build
      only what they recognise as theirs.
    </p>
    <ul class="rows">
      <li>
        <b>Discovery that gets real answers</b>
        Structured questions, native language, and permission to answer badly. The output is usable
        input rather than a polite shrug.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Scope agreed up front</b>
        A written boundary on what proceeds without a check-in. That is a RACI chart written for a
        human being.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Adoption by construction</b>
        She owns the answers, so she recognises what gets built. Nobody has to be sold on their own
        business.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="learned">
    <h2><span class="n">06 / What I learned</span>Mostly about who holds the pen.</h2>
    <ul class="rows">
      <li>
        <b>Translate the form, not just the words</b>
        A translated English worksheet is still an English worksheet. Leading in Spanish and
        annotating in English changes who the document is for.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Lower the cost of answering</b>
        Audio, photos, a shrug, and multiple choice are all valid inputs. Insisting on written
        paragraphs filters for confidence in a second language, not for good answers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Write the boundary before the build</b>
        Decision rights are cheap to agree while nothing exists yet, and expensive to argue about
        once something does.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Helping is not the same as taking over</b>
        The most useful thing I did on this project was design a way to stay out of the decisions
        that were never mine.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
